ition to English representations of one of Japan’s most imaginative, controversial novelists” — Times Literary Supplement\n“Lyrical, dramatic and unforgettable…Highly recommended!” — Shiny New Books\nPraise for Jinochiro Tanizaki:\n“Junichiro Tanizaki may well prove to be the outstanding Japanese novelist of this century” — New York Times Book Review\n \n“Japan’s great modern novelist. Tanizaki createda lifelong series of ingenious variations on a dominant theme: the power of love to energize and destroy” — Chicago Tribune\n \n“Tanizaki writes with an unabashed sensuality” — John Updike\n\n[Button: See More ]\n\n### Product Details Image\n\nISBN 9781782278092\n\nPublished on Sep 26, 2023\n\nPublished by Pushkin Press\n\nPages 224\n\nDimensions 4-11/16 x 6-1/2\n\nThe Siren’s Lament\n\nBy Jun’Ichiro Tanizaki\n\nTranslated by Bryan Karetnyk--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE CATHOLIC UNIVERSITY OF AMERICA (https://juliobermudez.com/courses/bermudez-silvestrin/syllabus.pdf)\nciteturn33search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; recommended texts (i.e., articles, book chapters, etc.) and media content (e.g., video, music, etc.). ... The Immateriality of Materiality, Claudio Silvestrin (in press)\nRequired & Recommended Texts \nWhile the following books will be used throughout the semester, there will be other required or \nrecommended texts (i.e., articles, book chapters, etc.) and media content (e.g., video, music, etc.). Please, \nrefer to each design assignment for specifics (to be handed out separately). \nClaudio Silvestrin, Franco Bertoni translated by Lucinda Byatt (Birkhäuser 1999)  \nEye Claudio, Claudio Silvestrin ( Booth-Clibborn, 2002)  \nThe Immateriality of Materiality, Claudio Silvestrin (in press) \nThinking Architecture, Peter Zumthor (Berlin: Birkhause Publishers 1998) \nVoluntary Simplicity, Duane Elgin (New York: William Morrow, 1993)  \nBeginner’s mind : Design Mind, Julio Bermudez (published